# [House Robber III](https://leetcode.com/problems/house-robber-iii/)

## Approach
- Use a recursive function that returns a pair of values for each node.
- `first`: Maximum money that can be robbed when the current node is **not robbed**.
- `second`: Maximum money that can be robbed when the current node **is robbed**.
- If the current node is not robbed, take the maximum result from each child.
- If the current node is robbed, add its value to the results where both children are not robbed.
- Return the maximum of the two values for the root.

## Code
```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    pair<int, int> solve(TreeNode* root) {
        if (!root) return {0, 0};

        pair<int, int> left_node = solve(root->left);
        pair<int, int> right_node = solve(root->right);

        int neigh = max(left_node.first, left_node.second)
                  + max(right_node.first, right_node.second);

        int not_neigh = root->val + left_node.first + right_node.first;

        return {neigh, not_neigh};
    }

    int rob(TreeNode* root) {
        auto [max1, max2] = solve(root);
        return max(max1, max2);
    }
};
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Time Complexity:** \(O(n)\), where \(n\) is the number of nodes, because each node is visited once.
- **Space Complexity:** \(O(h)\), where \(h\) is the height of the tree, due to the recursive call stack.
